1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,833
YA-NEC video course - lesson 1: 14 MHz dipole, three ways.

2
00:00:04,883 --> 00:00:11,250
Lesson 1: a 14 MHz dipole built three ways. First way: the Dipoles calculator.

3
00:00:11,300 --> 00:00:17,390
Calculators tab, Dipoles, type half-wave.

4
00:00:17,440 --> 00:00:23,713
Frequency 14.150 MHz, 2 mm wire, 10 m above ground.

5
00:00:23,763 --> 00:00:33,686
The formula gives 10.08 m, but the real-time calculation below shows minimum SWR at 14.52 MHz: the wire is too short.

6
00:00:33,736 --> 00:00:42,762
'Bring to resonance (NEC)' lets the engine find the right length.

7
00:00:42,812 --> 00:00:53,485
10.36 m: the NEC check gives 72.7 + j0 ohm, 7.1 dBi. Shortening factor 0.978.

8
00:00:53,535 --> 00:01:00,618
'Open as model': the dipole goes into the 3D structure, already over average ground at 10 m.

9
00:01:00,668 --> 00:01:10,912
72.7 + j0.01 ohm, SWR 1.45, 7.10 dBi at 29.5 degrees of elevation.

10
00:01:10,962 --> 00:01:26,639
Second way: by hand, with numbers. New, then 'New without saving'.

11
00:01:26,689 --> 00:01:38,649
Frequency 14.150 MHz, Add wire: Y from -5.18 to +5.18 m, Z = 10 m, radius 1 mm, 25 segments.

12
00:01:38,699 --> 00:01:50,625
Apply, then the source in the centre: w1c.

13
00:01:50,675 --> 00:01:56,492
Without ground the height does not matter: this is the free-space dipole.

14
00:01:56,542 --> 00:02:03,836
Ground: Average. The wire is already at 10 m.

15
00:02:03,886 --> 00:02:11,802
72.9 - j0.04 ohm, 7.09 dBi: the same result as the calculator.

16
00:02:11,852 --> 00:02:28,949
Third way: with variables. Variables tab: L = 10.36 (length) and H = 10 (height).

17
00:02:28,999 --> 00:02:44,336
In the wire table we write formulas: Y1 = -L/2, Z1 = H, Y2 = L/2, Z2 = H.

18
00:02:44,386 --> 00:02:49,736
Same antenna, but now length and height are two numbers to change.

19
00:02:49,786 --> 00:02:59,067
H = 5 and Apply: the whole model follows.

20
00:02:59,117 --> 00:03:10,549
At 5 m: 82 + j29 ohm, 5.84 dBi, maximum at 69 degrees. Back to H = 10.

21
00:03:10,599 --> 00:03:19,957
Wire material: so far 'Lossless'. Let us choose Copper.

22
00:03:20,007 --> 00:03:32,433
Copper: 73.8 ohm and 7.04 dBi, only 0.05 dB less: a 2 mm copper wire wastes very little.

23
00:03:32,483 --> 00:03:42,932
Aluminium 6063: 74.1 ohm, 7.02 dBi. Back to Lossless.

24
00:03:42,982 --> 00:03:49,398
Extended kernel (EK): a more exact formula for thick wires or very short segments.

25
00:03:49,448 --> 00:03:59,095
For a thin wire nothing changes (72.9 - j0.04 ohm): EK is for thick tubes, when radius and segment length are similar.

26
00:03:59,145 --> 00:04:06,029
Calculator, numbers or variables: same dipole, choose the way that suits you. 73 de IC8TEM
